    <code code="10154-3" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Chief complaint Narrative - Reported"/>
    <title>Chief Complaint</title>
    <text><paragraph>Burning on passing urine and frequency for two days, with the tingling in her fingers back. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Ms. du Pré called the clinic on the second day of burning on passing urine, frequency and urgency, and was seen the same day. Since yesterday she has felt feverish (37.8 °C at home) and the tingling in the fingers of her left hand, which had almost gone, has come back, with the fingers feeling a little clumsy on the fingerboard this morning. There is no new weakness, no unsteadiness, no change in vision and no back or loin pain. She has had no vaginal discharge and her last period was two weeks ago. She completed the first ocrelizumab course seven weeks ago. She is afraid this is a new relapse and asked whether she needs steroids again. She has had no thoughts of self-harm. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Two days ago</td></tr><tr><td>Severity</td><td>Burning, frequency and urgency; low fever; no loin pain</td></tr><tr><td>Context</td><td>Seven weeks after the first ocrelizumab course; infections are more common on anti-CD20 therapy</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>Old tingling in the left fingers returned with the fever; no new deficit</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

