    <title>Problems</title>
    <text><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Clinical status</th><th>Verification</th><th>Code</th><th>Provenance</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr ID="problem-1">
    <td>Numbness, tingling and loss of position sense in the fingers of both hands for three weeks, worse on the left, with clumsiness that makes her lose her place on the fingerboard, an electric feeling down the spine on bending the neck (Lhermitte&apos;s sign), and fatigue; a similar month of tingling in the fingers 18 months ago settled on its own. A lesion of the cervical spinal cord must be found: MRI of the brain and spine with contrast booked this week</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>R20.2</td><td>S — Synthetic presenting problem anchored to her documented loss of sensation in her fingers and fatigue. Superseded by the diagnosis at the second visit.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-2">
    <td>Relapsing-remitting multiple sclerosis, active: a current relapse (partial cervical myelitis at C3 to C4 with sensory loss and clumsiness of the hands) after an earlier sensory episode; dissemination in space and time on MRI, with an enhancing cord lesion and an enhancing brain lesion; CSF-specific oligoclonal bands; aquaporin-4 antibody negative; meets the 2017 McDonald criteria and the 2024 revision; synthetic modern detail linked to her documented diagnosis</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>G35.A</td><td>S — Synthetic course, imaging and laboratory detail; linked to her documented diagnosis in October 1973.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-3">
    <td>Vitamin D deficiency: 25-hydroxyvitamin D 16 ng/mL; replacement to correct the deficiency, not as a treatment for multiple sclerosis</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>E55.9</td><td>S — Synthetic finding.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-4">
    <td>Acute uncomplicated cystitis (Escherichia coli) seven weeks after the first ocrelizumab course, with a pseudo-relapse: two days of returning tingling in the fingers with a low fever and no new neurological deficit; resolved</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>N30.00</td><td>S — Synthetic intercurrent infection; not a historical claim.</td>
  </tr></tbody></table></text>

