  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute uncomplicated cystitis with a pseudo-relapse (N30.00)</content>. Acute cystitis: the dipstick shows leukocyte esterase and nitrites, and the culture later grew Escherichia coli sensitive to nitrofurantoin; white cells 10.2 and CRP 14 mg/L. There is no loin pain and the fever is low, so this is not pyelonephritis. The return of old tingling with a fever and no new sign is a pseudo-relapse, caused by the infection and the raised temperature acting on an old lesion, not new inflammation, so steroids are not needed. [S]<list><item>Nitrofurantoin 100 mg twice daily for 5 days.</item><item>Plenty of fluids; acetaminophen for fever.</item><item>If the tingling has not settled within a day or two of the fever going, or any new symptom appears, call: that would need a neurological review and MRI.</item><item>Call at once for loin pain, rigors or vomiting.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Relapsing-remitting multiple sclerosis on ocrelizumab (G35.A)</content>. First course completed seven weeks ago; hand function had nearly recovered before this illness. [S]<list><item>Re-baseline MRI and review in four weeks, as booked.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Multiple sclerosis in adults: management (NICE guideline NG220) (2022); Gupta K, et al. International clinical practice guidelines for the treatment of acute uncomplicated cystitis and pyelonephritis in women: a 2010 update by the IDSA and ESCMID. Clin Infect Dis 2011;52(5):e103-e120 (PMID 21292654) (2011)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Phone call in two days. Review with the re-baseline MRI in four weeks. Call for loin pain, high fever or any new neurological symptom. Next scheduled visit 2026-07-13: three months on ocrelizumab: re-baseline MRI, B-cell depletion, fatigue, vitamin D and hand function. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
